Array Objects do not create individual objects objects, only one object
that holds references to other objects

Recursion takes up an inefficient amount of memory in most cases
A Stack Data Structure has a push section (writing onto the stack) 
and a pop section (removal from stack)

Iteration is preferable to a Recursive Structure when on a Linear Time Scale
Recursion works when there's a repeated pattern within a system

The Push Section Builds the Stack, once the Recursion ends at the Basecase
The Pop Section Removes the function calls from the Stack, returning the final value

At every section of the Stack is a Program State, Recursion utilizes these Program States
to save local variable states, before popping them off the Stack

when it comes to big(O), Iteration is more Memory Efficient then Recursion
Since Iteration's Stack Space is only one Stackframe,
Meanwhile Recursion's Stack Space is O(n) Stackframes.

There's the possibility of running out of Stack Space when using Recursion
if the data set is too large